          ],
          "section": "CONE MODE HANDLING"
        },
        {
          "source_id": "P1-sparse-v2.43.0",
          "lines": [
            377,
            425
          ],
          "section": "CONE PATTERN SET"
        }
      ],
      "conditions": "Git 2.43 cone directory selection; tracked superproject working-tree files. Parent patterns are nonrecursive; recursive patterns select the chosen subtree. Non-cone patterns have different semantics.",
      "assessment": "SUPPORTED"
    },
    {
      "id": "C2",
      "claim": "Sparse checkout is working-tree scoping, distinct from partial-clone object filtering; neither cited definition proves a selected-file-size bound on total traffic.",
      "candidate": {
        "path": "ER12_RUNTIME/runs/B-FINAL-G-02/treatment/stages/role/final-section.md",
        "line": 7,
        "exact_text": "Sparse checkout selects which tracked files appear in the working tree; it does not by itself filter object transfer or bound total bytes."
      },
      "primary": [
        {
          "source_id": "P1-sparse-v2.43.0",
          "lines": [
            18,
            31
          ],
          "section": "DESCRIPTION"
        },
        {
          "source_id": "P1-sparse-v2.43.0",
          "lines": [
            201,
            214
          ],
          "section": "SPARSE CHECKOUT internals"
        },
        {
          "source_id": "P2-clone-v2.43.0",
          "lines": [
            174,
            188
          ],
          "section": "--sparse; --filter"
        }
      ],
      "conditions": "Object transfer versus worktree materialization are different operations. blob:none delays blobs until Git needs them. Network units are bytes transferred for a defined workload; no workload/server/protocol measurement exists. The no-bound conclusion is inference, not a quoted universal numerical limit.",
      "assessment": "SUPPORTED"
    },
    {
      "id": "C3",
      "claim": "--depth=1 truncates commit history, so the full-history policy rejects it. Blob filtering and full-depth commits are compatible concepts.",
      "candidate": {
        "path": "ER12_RUNTIME/runs/B-FINAL-G-02/treatment/stages/role/final-section.md",
        "line": 17,
        "exact_text": "`--depth=1` creates a shallow clone with history truncated to one commit"
      },
      "primary": [
        {
          "source_id": "P2-clone-v2.43.0",
          "lines": [
            245,
            268
          ],
          "section": "--depth; --shallow-since; --shallow-exclude; --single-branch"
        },
        {
          "source_id": "P2-clone-v2.43.0",
          "lines": [
            25,
            40
          ],
          "section": "default clone branch histories"
        },
        {
          "source_id": "P2-clone-v2.43.0",
          "lines": [
            180,
            188
          ],
          "section": "partial clone filter"
        }
      ],
      "conditions": "Commit-graph depth, not present-tree completeness or all historical blob payloads. Full history is a product constraint for the intended clone, not a claim to retrieve unreachable/deleted commits or all independent submodule history. Source repositories may themselves be shallow (see P2 157–160).",
      "assessment": "SUPPORTED"
    },
    {
      "id": "C4",
      "claim": "Merge/rebase and dirty/conflicted files can materialize or retain extra paths; reapply follows cleanup, and disable restores ordinary tracked working-tree coverage.",
      "candidate": {
        "path": "ER12_RUNTIME/runs/B-FINAL-G-02/treatment/stages/role/final-section.md",
        "line": 9,
        "exact_text": "the helper then runs `git sparse-checkout reapply` after the user resolves, commits, or undoes those changes"
      },
      "primary": [
        {
          "source_id": "P1-sparse-v2.43.0",
          "lines": [
            99,
            116
          ],
          "section": "reapply; disable"
        },
        {
          "source_id": "P1-sparse-v2.43.0",
          "lines": [
            174,
            196
          ],
          "section": "EXAMPLES"
        }
      ],
      "conditions": "reapply is not a promise to force away unresolved/modified files. inspect/preserve/stop is the helper product rule, not a Git guarantee; no reset/clean or destructive automatic recovery is prescribed. disable restores tracked superproject paths, not deleted local user data or uninitialized submodules.",
      "assessment": "SUPPORTED"
    },
    {
      "id": "C5",
      "claim": "Sparse index is optional, default off, experimental, and concerns index size/performance rather than stopping network downloads.",
      "candidate": {
        "path": "ER12_RUNTIME/runs/B-FINAL-G-02/treatment/stages/role/final-section.md",
        "line": 20,
        "exact_text": "A sparse index is an optional, experimental, default-off index-size and performance feature"
      },
      "primary": [
        {
          "source_id": "P1-sparse-v2.43.0",
          "lines": [
            73,
            91
          ],
          "section": "set --[no-]sparse-index"
        }
      ],
      "conditions": "Git 2.43 documented default; cone supports the sparse-index option, non-cone does not. Benefits are command dependent; older Git/external tools can be incompatible. Candidate rejects it as mandatory network prevention, not as every possible future local optimization.",
      "assessment": "SUPPORTED"
    },
